  "meta": {
    "title": "Fever Reef Redux: Vibrio Storms the Mucus Layer at Heron Island",
    "date": "2026-09-24",
    "lesson": "When corals bleach in a marine heatwave, they don't just lose their algal color partners (zooxanthellae) - their surface mucus, normally a nutrient-poor, well-defended barrier, becomes a nutrient-rich, undefended target. This scenario models the reef flat at Heron Island during a bleaching event: warm, shallow water breaks down the coral's protective mucopolysaccharide layer, releasing chitin-rich mucus glycoproteins that opportunistic Vibrio-type bacteria rush to exploit. Play as a chitinolytic colonist racing to break down mucus particles (chitin/glycoprotein-dominant) before ciliate grazers and viral predation cut you down, while EPS matrix production offers refuge in the increasingly crowded, warming water. The lesson: coral disease outbreaks are often less about a single pathogen invading and more about a stressed host's defenses collapsing, opening a feeding frenzy for generalist opportunists already present in reef water.",
    "citation": "Ushijima et al. 2020, Vibrio coralliilyticus pathogenesis in thermally stressed corals; Great Barrier Reef 2016 mass bleaching event studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "2016 Great Barrier Reef mass bleaching, Heron Island reef flat, Vibrio coralliilyticus mucus-layer opportunism"
  },
